From 456564e7e30fc6c13aa482f744cb1cc52ff3ee22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 13:41:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] Draw labels through coverage masks with built-in bitmap
 fonts

Pango costs a font scan at start-up and shaping on every key draw, and pulls glib, harfbuzz and freetype into a 64 MiB handheld's keyboard process. Every label is now a coverage mask (1, 4 or 8 bits per pixel, rows padded to 4 bytes, with width, height and bearing) and one blitter, mask_blit(), draws it in the key colour into the RGB565 or 32-bit buffer. 1 bpp is a masked fill; 4 and 8 bpp go through a lookup table of 0..256 weights, so there is no division per pixel on a CPU without an FPU. The blitter clips to the buffer and to the key's inner box.

The blitter takes its memory, stride and size from the cairo surface that is actually drawn into, not from the surface size in drwsurf. When setup_buffer fails part way the size is already the new one while the surface is still the old one, and trusting the size would write past the old mapping. The draw is skipped when the surface has no memory.

The build option picks the backend: the meson option text and the Makefile variable TEXT, each bitmap or pango, with bitmap the default.

- bitmap: the X.Org misc-fixed fonts 6x13 and 10x20, converted from their BDF sources by tools/bdf2c.py into bmfont-data.c (ISO 8859-1 plus every other code point the mobintl and deskintl layouts use that the font has: 618 and 533 glyphs, 46 KB of masks). The output is committed rather than generated by meson so the OE build needs no network and the target build needs no python; tools/regen-fonts.sh fetches the BDFs from a pinned upstream revision, verifies their sha256 and the licence, and regenerates it. The font is the largest whose cell is at most 85 percent of the inner key height and whose label fits the width. A glyph the font lacks is drawn as a box and logged once per code point, never skipped silently.
- pango: each label is rendered once, on first use, with pango and cairo into a CAIRO_FORMAT_A8 surface, copied into an 8 bpp mask and cached per key size, then drawn through the same blitter. The layout wraps to the key's inner width and is ellipsized at its inner height, as the cairo drawing it replaces did. Only this build links pango and pangocairo, and so harfbuzz.

The bitmap build links no pango, pangocairo or harfbuzz, and wvkbd itself has no direct fontconfig dependency. libcairo may still pull in fontconfig transitively depending on how cairo is built, so whether the target has fontconfig is decided by the cairo recipe.

Both BDF files carry the licence in their header: COPYRIGHT "Public domain font.  Share and enjoy."

Shift, caps lock, backspace and enter stay vector shapes drawn with cairo, so cairo remains a dependency. pango/pangocairo.h (via drw.h) used to supply signal.h, stdlib.h and string.h to main.c and keyboard.c by accident; they are now included explicitly.

Tests: the blitter for each depth with exact pixels (clipping at buffer edges, padding rows, stride padding untouched, alpha handling per format), the glyph lookup, the missing-glyph box and its log-once behaviour, font choice, and for the pango build a test that a label renders to a non-empty mask and that a long label wraps and ellipsizes. test-draw samples a pixel of key colour instead of a fixed offset, since the label can cover it on short keys, checks that a key's pixels differ with and without its label and are identical without it twice, and draws with a surface smaller than the recorded size between guard bytes to check nothing outside the surface is written.
